# nestrs — the demo's complete configuration, committed on purpose.
#
# The demo holds no real secret: its keypair is the repo's dev keypair, which
# `auth` signs with in development and in its own suite, and its OAuth
# credentials are fixtures with no provider behind them. Nothing here is worth
# hiding, so nothing is split out — `git clone` then `nestrs run` works with no
# copy step and no file to prime.
# A real app keeps the secret-shaped half in a git-ignored `.env.local`; that is
# what `nestrs new` scaffolds. See https://nestrs.dev/configuration/env-cascade/.
#
# Only OVERRIDES live here, not every setting: anything omitted falls back to the
# in-code default of the owning `#[config]` struct. Real environment variables
# always win over this file.
#
# Every app reads this file. What identifies one app — its port, the audience it
# requires, the issuer's signing key — lives in that app's own `apps/<app>/.env`,
# read over these under a declared development or test profile only.
#
# Precedence (highest first):
#   real env  >  apps/<app>/.env.<NESTRS_ENV>.local  >  apps/<app>/.env.local
#   >  apps/<app>/.env.<NESTRS_ENV>  >  apps/<app>/.env
#   >  .env.<NESTRS_ENV>.local  >  .env.local  >  .env.<NESTRS_ENV>  >  .env

# No database, Redis or object store here: this file is read in every profile,
# production included. The dev container's services — and the credentials its
# RustFS accepts — live in `.env.development` and `.env.test`, read only under
# those profiles; a deployment names its own.

# JWT public key (EdDSA) — the half every app verifies with, of the repo's dev
# keypair, public by design: its private half is committed for development, so
# anyone with the repo can mint tokens the demo accepts. That is the point of
# a demo, and the reason this file is not a model for a deployment. Only `auth`
# signs, so its private half sits in `apps/auth/.env` alone; the other apps'
# suites sign with a key of their own, which the harness installs in place of
# this one. Generate your own pair before running any of this for real:
#   printf 'NESTRS_AUTHN__PRIVATE_KEY="%s"\n' \
#     "$(openssl genpkey -algorithm ed25519 | sed ':a;N;$!ba;s/\n/\\n/g')"
# and supply both halves through the real environment.
NESTRS_AUTHN__PUBLIC_KEY="-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\nMCowBQYDK2VwAyEAHfPOjd2Y3m1BLM5nBJBMZFAlfWt69WL1NY8XyYeGfeo=\n-----END PUBLIC KEY-----\n"
# What holds: `auth` mints every access token for the one resource it was asked
# for (the RFC 8707 `resource` parameter) — one its client is registered for, or
# one a sign-in may name, below — and stamps its issuer URL (`.env.development`).
# Each app requires its own origin as the token's `aud` (`apps/<app>/.env`), and
# the `assistant`, an OAuth 2.1 resource server (RFC 9728), its resource: a token
# minted for one app is refused by every other.

# Registered clients of the `auth` issuer's `/token` endpoint (the
# `client_credentials` grant), the authorization server's registry
# (`nest_rs::oauth::server`). A JSON array; each client authenticates with HTTP
# Basic (`client_id:client_secret`), acts as its `payload`'s org and roles, and is
# granted at most its `scopes`, each one of posts:read, posts:write, audio:transcode.
# Its tokens are minted for one of its `resources` — the api, the assistant, the
# live app: the one its request names, or its only one. Its `client_id` is its
# tokens' `sub`, so one a UUID parses (a user's) fails the boot.
NESTRS_OAUTH__SERVER__CLIENTS=[{"client_id":"demo-service","client_secret":"demo-service-secret","payload":{"org_id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-000000000000","roles":["admin"]},"scopes":["posts:read","posts:write","audio:transcode"],"resources":["http://localhost:3002","http://localhost:3003","http://localhost:3004"]}]
# The resources a user's sign-in — the password login or a social provider's,
# whose `/social/{provider}/authorize?resource=` carries it to the callback —
# may name in its `resource`; one naming none is minted for the first, the api.
NESTRS_SIGN_IN__RESOURCES=http://localhost:3002,http://localhost:3003,http://localhost:3004

# OpenAPI document identity for the `api` app (title, version and description
# are its package's, `apps/api/Cargo.toml`); the license is the demo's own, its
# SPDX identifier.
NESTRS_OPENAPI__LICENSE_NAME=MIT
NESTRS_OPENAPI__LICENSE_IDENTIFIER=MIT
